---
import SpeakerNotes from '@/components/slides/SpeakerNotes.astro'

export const prerender = true

const enableSpeakerNotes = import.meta.env.DEV
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Why We Should View Docs as a Product</title>
    <link rel="icon" href="./favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="./vendor/reveal/dist/reveal.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="./vendor/fonts/geist/index.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="./vendor/fonts/geist-mono/index.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="./css/tokens.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="./css/base.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="./css/utilities.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="./css/code.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="./css/deck.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="./css/components.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="./css/reveal-overrides.css" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="./css/talk-chrome.css" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="reveal">
      <div class="slides">
        <section class="deck-slide--cover">
          <div class="deck-cover">
            <div class="deck-cover__lead">
              <div class="deck-cover__copy">
                <p class="vs-eyebrow">Documentation in the Age of AI</p>
                <h1 class="vs-title">Why We Should View Docs as a Product</h1>
              </div>

              <img
                class="deck-qr deck-qr--large deck-cover__qr"
                src="./qr.svg"
                alt="Scan to open the talk page"
              />
            </div>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 0:45</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> AI changes how people reach documentation, but
              the docs still need to be right.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> Some people open the docs themselves. Others ask
              an agent that reads the docs for them. Either way, the answer starts
              with the same source, so someone has to own it and keep it accurate
              and current. That's why I think docs should be managed like a product.
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>

        <section class="deck-slide--chapter">
          <div class="deck-stage">
            <p class="vs-eyebrow">Start with the user</p>
            <h1 class="deck-question">Who reads your docs?</h1>
            <p class="fragment deck-answer">And what counts as “reading”?</p>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 0:35</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> Start with the person and what they need to
              do.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> The obvious reader is a person trying to understand
              or use the product. They might read the page themselves, or they might
              ask an agent to read it for them. The question is the same: what are
              they trying to get done?
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>

        <section>
          <div class="deck-stage deck-stage--pipeline">
            <p class="vs-eyebrow">When people read docs</p>
            <h1 class="vs-title vs-title--compact">
              The documentation page was the experience.
            </h1>

            <docs-pipeline
              class="deck-pipeline-single"
              stage="search"
              stage-label="Search"
              steps='[
                { "kind": "human", "label": "Human" },
                { "kind": "search", "label": "Search" },
                { "kind": "docs", "label": "Docs" },
                { "kind": "product", "label": "Product" }
              ]'
            ></docs-pipeline>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 0:45</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> This is a simple model of a familiar path, not
              a history of every way people use documentation.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> A person searches, opens the docs, decides whether
              the answer works, and moves into the product. The docs page is where
              the team presents the explanation. The next slide changes that part
              of the path.
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>

        <section>
          <div class="deck-stage deck-stage--pipeline">
            <p class="vs-eyebrow">How people use docs now</p>
            <h1 class="vs-title vs-title--compact">
              The path now has an intermediary.
            </h1>
            <p class="fragment deck-answer" data-fragment-index="1">
              A person asks. An agent consults the docs. The person acts.
            </p>

            <docs-pipeline
              stage="product"
              stage-label="Agent-mediated"
              steps='[
                { "kind": "human", "label": "Human asks" },
                { "kind": "ai", "label": "AI consults" },
                { "kind": "docs", "label": "Docs" },
                { "kind": "ai", "label": "AI answers" },
                { "kind": "product", "label": "Product" }
              ]'
            ></docs-pipeline>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 0:55</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> An agent changes the route to the answer, not
              the goal.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> We still want to help a person use the product.
              Now they may ask an agent instead of opening the docs themselves. The
              agent checks the documentation, answers the question, and the person
              acts. The docs are still the source. The agent is now in the middle.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Supporting example:</strong> In Gauge's 500-run sample of
              coding-agent workflows, documentation accounted for 55% of recorded
              web fetches. Gauge has not published the detailed methodology, so use
              this as an illustrative vendor observation, not a general statistic.
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>

        <section>
          <div class="deck-stage deck-stage--evidence">
            <p class="vs-eyebrow">The wider web</p>

            <div class="deck-stat deck-stat--traffic">
              <traffic-split
                claim-id="THALES-WEB-BOTS-001"
                human='{ "label": "Human traffic", "percentage": "47%" }'
                ai='{ "label": "Automated traffic", "percentage": "53%" }'
                context="In Thales's 2025 analysis, automated traffic exceeded human traffic."
              ></traffic-split>
            </div>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 1:05</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> Thales found more automated traffic than human
              traffic in 2025, but that doesn't mean AI traffic was the majority.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> Before we narrow this to documentation, look at
              the wider web. Thales found that automated requests made up 53% of web
              traffic in its full-year 2025 analysis. Human traffic was 47%.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Careful:</strong> That 53% includes all bots, not only LLM crawlers
              or agents. In a separate 2025 measurement across its customer base,
              Cloudflare found that identified AI bots averaged 4.2% of HTML requests.
              It counted dual-purpose Googlebot separately at 4.5%. The next two slides
              focus specifically on documentation platforms and agent requests.
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>

        <section>
          <div class="deck-stage deck-stage--evidence">
            <p class="vs-eyebrow">Docs traffic · GitBook</p>

            <traffic-split
              claim-id="CYC26-DOCS-AI-001"
              class="deck-stat deck-stat--traffic"
              human='{ "label": "Human requests", "percentage": "48.2%" }'
              ai='{ "label": "AI-agent requests", "percentage": "51.8%" }'
              context="On GitBook platforms, AI agents already request pages about as often as people do."
            ></traffic-split>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 1:10</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> In one GitBook sample, agents made slightly
              more of the requests GitBook called "intentional reads."
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> GitBook grouped page requests into human, AI agent,
              crawler, and other bot traffic. It removed the crawler groups before
              publishing this split. Over that one week, AI agent requests were 51.8%
              and human requests were 48.2%.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Careful:</strong> These are page requests, not people, sessions,
              completed tasks, or proof that someone understood the page. It's also
              only one week of traffic. I don't want to overstate it, but it shows
              that people aren't the only ones reading docs anymore.
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>

        <section>
          <div class="deck-stage deck-stage--evidence">
            <p class="vs-eyebrow">Docs traffic · Mintlify</p>

            <traffic-split
              claim-id="MINTLIFY-AI-001"
              class="deck-stat deck-stat--traffic"
              human='{ "label": "Human browser page loads", "percentage": "105M", "share": "34%" }'
              ai='{ "label": "Agent HTTP requests", "percentage": "213M", "share": "66%" }'
              context="On Mintlify platforms, agent requests already outnumber human traffic two to one."
            ></traffic-split>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 1:00</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> Mintlify reported twice as many agent requests
              as human page loads in July 2026.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> Mintlify counted 213 million agent HTTP requests
              and 105 million human browser page loads across its hosted documentation.
              That is a reported 66% agent share, up from 15.2% at the start of the
              year.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Careful:</strong> These numbers measure different events. Human
              activity uses client-side page loads, while agent activity uses server-side
              requests. GitBook and Mintlify also use different methods, so we shouldn't
              combine them into a single trend.
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>

        <section class="deck-slide--chapter">
          <div class="deck-stage deck-stage--evidence">
            <p class="vs-eyebrow">Why quality matters</p>
            <h1 class="vs-title">Bad docs become bad actions.</h1>
            <p class="fragment deck-answer">
              The agent follows the wrong path. The task fails. The user feels
              the frustration.
            </p>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 1:15</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> When an agent follows bad documentation, the
              user's task can fail.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> A person can sometimes spot an incomplete page
              and work around it. An agent may follow the documented path with confidence.
              If that path is wrong, stale, or missing an important constraint, the
              action fails, and the user is the one dealing with it. That makes documentation
              quality and ownership part of the product experience.
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>

        <section class="deck-slide--chapter">
          <div class="deck-stage">
            <p class="vs-eyebrow">Docs as product</p>
            <h1 class="vs-title vs-title--compact">
              What that looks like in practice.
            </h1>

            <ol class="deck-pillars">
              <li class="fragment" data-fragment-index="1">
                <span class="deck-pillars__num">01</span>
                <span class="deck-pillars__body">
                  <strong>Own it</strong>
                  <span
                    >A product owner for docs; PMs and engineers own the truth</span
                  >
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="fragment" data-fragment-index="2">
                <span class="deck-pillars__num">02</span>
                <span class="deck-pillars__body">
                  <strong>Draft it efficiently</strong>
                  <span
                    >Low-friction drafting: agentic tooling, human in the loop</span
                  >
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="fragment" data-fragment-index="3">
                <span class="deck-pillars__num">03</span>
                <span class="deck-pillars__body">
                  <strong>Keep it correct</strong>
                  <span
                    >Automated checks in CI (spectral for OpenAPI) before
                    publish</span
                  >
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="fragment" data-fragment-index="4">
                <span class="deck-pillars__num">04</span>
                <span class="deck-pillars__body">
                  <strong>Make it reachable</strong>
                  <span
                    >llms.txt, MCP, and pages you can copy as Markdown (<code
                      >.md</code
                    >)</span
                  >
                </span>
              </li>
              <li class="fragment" data-fragment-index="5">
                <span class="deck-pillars__num">05</span>
                <span class="deck-pillars__body">
                  <strong>Learn and improve</strong>
                  <span
                    >Analytics, user feedback, and a maintenance roadmap</span
                  >
                </span>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 0:50</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> Treating docs as a product describes how the
              work should run.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> If bad docs can lead to bad actions, they deserve
              the same care as the rest of the product. Someone owns the docs system.
              Feature teams own what is true. AI can help with drafts, automated checks
              help keep content correct, and the docs need to work for people and
              agents. Then we learn from real use and plan what to improve. The next
              few slides take those ideas one at a time.
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>

        <section>
          <div class="deck-stage deck-stage--detail">
            <p class="vs-eyebrow">Docs as product</p>
            <h1 class="vs-title">
              The system and content need different owners.
            </h1>

            <div class="fragment deck-two-up">
              <div class="vs-node deck-node-copy">
                <span class="vs-node__label">Docs product owner</span>
                <strong>Owns the system</strong>
                <span
                  >Infrastructure, publishing, agentic authoring tools,
                  discovery, and usability</span
                >
              </div>
              <div class="vs-node deck-node-copy">
                <span class="vs-node__label">Feature PMs + engineers</span>
                <strong>Own the content</strong>
                <span
                  >Accuracy, initial drafts, and maintenance as the product
                  changes</span
                >
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 1:10</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> The documentation system and the product facts
              need different owners.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> The docs product owner maintains the infrastructure,
              publishing workflow, authoring tools, and the paths that make the docs
              easy to find and use. PMs and engineers own the feature content. They
              create or verify the first draft, make sure it is accurate, and keep
              it current as the product changes.
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>

        <section>
          <div class="deck-stage deck-stage--detail">
            <p class="vs-eyebrow">Docs as product</p>
            <h1 class="vs-title">Start with a user and a task.</h1>
            <p class="fragment deck-answer">
              Then measure whether they can complete it.
            </p>

            <p class="fragment deck-equation">
              <span>A request</span>
              <strong aria-label="does not equal">≠</strong>
              <span>A completed task</span>
            </p>
            <p class="fragment deck-answer">
              The same task must succeed for a human and an agent.
            </p>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 2:15</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> Traffic tells us that someone reached the docs.
              It doesn't tell us whether they finished the task.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> "Developers" is too broad to be a useful user
              model. Someone evaluating a product, someone responding to an incident,
              and someone handling a breaking change have different jobs. Name the
              user, name the task, and decide what success looks like. Then test that
              task with a person or an agent. Watch what happens and use the result
              to make a decision, such as improving navigation, explaining a prerequisite,
              or removing a stale example. A page request alone can't tell us whether
              someone found the right version, understood a constraint, or completed
              the task.
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>

        <section>
          <div class="deck-stage deck-stage--detail">
            <p class="vs-eyebrow">A practical workflow</p>
            <h1 class="vs-title vs-title--compact">
              Use AI to draft, not to decide.
            </h1>

            <div
              class="deck-process"
              aria-label="Draft, review, validate, publish, and maintain documentation workflow">
              <div class="vs-node">
                <span class="vs-node__label">AI tools</span>
                <strong>Draft</strong>
              </div>
              <div class="fragment vs-node">
                <span class="vs-node__label">Truth owners</span>
                <strong>Review</strong>
              </div>
              <div class="fragment vs-node">
                <span class="vs-node__label">CI checks</span>
                <strong>Validate</strong>
              </div>
              <div class="fragment vs-node">
                <span class="vs-node__label">Docs system</span>
                <strong>Publish</strong>
              </div>
              <div class="fragment vs-node">
                <span class="vs-node__label">Truth owners</span>
                <strong>Maintain</strong>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 0:50</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> Use AI for the first draft, but keep decisions
              and maintenance with the people who know the product.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> AI tools can turn product context into a first
              draft. The PMs and engineers closest to the feature review what is true.
              CI checks validate the change, such as Spectral linting for OpenAPI,
              and the docs system publishes the approved version. Those same PMs and
              engineers keep the content current. The docs product owner keeps the
              infrastructure and tooling running.
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>

        <section>
          <div class="deck-stage deck-stage--detail">
            <p class="vs-eyebrow">Human and agent access</p>
            <h1 class="vs-title vs-title--compact">
              Keep one source of truth. Serve every reader.
            </h1>

            <div
              class="deck-access-map"
              aria-label="One canonical documentation source with human and agent access paths">
              <div class="deck-access-map__human vs-node">
                <span class="vs-node__label">For people</span>
                <strong>Usable site docs</strong>
                <span
                  >Browse, search, ask AI, and view or copy pages as Markdown.</span
                >
              </div>
              <div class="deck-access-map__source vs-node">
                <span class="vs-node__label">Source of truth</span>
                <strong>Canonical documentation</strong>
                <span>Versioned, reviewed, maintained, with changelogs.</span>
              </div>
              <div class="deck-access-map__agent vs-node">
                <span class="vs-node__label">For agents</span>
                <strong>Useful access paths</strong>
                <span
                  ><code>llms.txt</code> · <code>llms-full.txt</code> · <code
                    >.md</code
                  > pages · MCP tools</span
                >
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 1:15</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> Keep one maintained source, then give people
              and agents ways to use it.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> People need readable pages, and they may want
              to copy a page as Markdown. Agents may use a curated llms dot txt file,
              a synchronized full export, or MCP tools when the task requires interaction.
              These are different paths into the same documentation, not separate
              documentation systems.
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>

        <section>
          <div class="deck-stage deck-stage--detail">
            <p class="vs-eyebrow">Docs as product</p>
            <h1 class="vs-title vs-title--compact">
              Let real use shape the roadmap.
            </h1>

            <div
              class="deck-loop"
              aria-label="Measure, listen, and improve documentation from real use">
              <div class="vs-node">
                <span class="vs-node__label">Measure</span>
                <strong>Track usage and outcomes</strong>
                <span
                  >Top searches, and whether AI chats actually answer, or come
                  up empty</span
                >
              </div>
              <div class="fragment vs-node">
                <span class="vs-node__label">Listen</span>
                <strong>Act on feedback</strong>
                <span>Capture it and ship it, crediting the community</span>
              </div>
              <div class="fragment vs-node">
                <span class="vs-node__label">Improve</span>
                <strong>Plan the work</strong>
                <span
                  >A maintenance roadmap that keeps every task doable, for
                  agents and people</span
                >
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 1:10</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> Let actual use and feedback decide what the
              docs team works on next.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> Look at what people and agents do. Which topics
              do they search for? Does the AI answer their questions, or does it come
              up empty? Capture feedback, act on it, and credit the community rather
              than calling out individuals. Put that work on a maintenance roadmap
              and keep checking whether both a person and an agent can complete the
              task. That is how docs stay useful instead of becoming a static artifact.
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>

        <section class="deck-slide--close">
          <div class="deck-close">
            <div class="deck-close__lead">
              <img
                class="deck-qr deck-qr--large"
                src="./qr.svg"
                alt="Scan to open the talk page"
              />

              <div class="deck-close__copy">
                <h1 class="vs-title vs-title--compact">
                  Treat documentation as a product.
                </h1>
                <p class="deck-closing-line">
                  Own the system. Maintain the truth. Measure task success.
                </p>
                <div class="deck-contact">
                  <img
                    class="deck-contact__photo"
                    src="./chris-nowicki.jpg"
                    alt="Chris Nowicki"
                  />
                  <div class="deck-contact__links">
                    <p class="deck-contact__name">Chris Nowicki</p>
                    <p class="deck-contact__role">
                      DevEx Engineer / Documentation Product Manager at <span
                        class="deck-contact__co">Commerce</span
                      >
                    </p>
                    <a
                      class="deck-contact__x"
                      href="https://x.com/iamwix"
                      aria-label="Chris Nowicki on X">@iamwix</a
                    >
                  </div>
                </div>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <SpeakerNotes>
            <p>Time: 0:30</p>
            <p>
              <strong>Point:</strong> Give the docs system an owner, keep product
              facts with the people closest to them, and measure whether users finish
              the task.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Say:</strong> Documentation shapes what people understand and
              what agents do. Treat it like a product. Give the system an owner, keep
              the content accurate, and measure whether the documentation helps the
              user finish what they came to do.
            </p>
          </SpeakerNotes>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>

    <script is:inline type="module" define:vars={{ enableSpeakerNotes }}>
      import './js/components.js'
      import Reveal from './vendor/reveal/dist/reveal.esm.js'

      // Expose the instance globally so tooling (e.g. decktape PDF export) and
      // the browser console can reach it; reveal's ESM build keeps it scoped.
      Object.assign(window, { Reveal })

      const plugins = []
      if (enableSpeakerNotes) {
        const { default: Notes } =
          await import('./vendor/reveal/plugin/notes/notes.esm.js')
        plugins.push(Notes)
      }

      // Pin a QR badge (links to the talk page) and an auto-incrementing page
      // number to the middle slides, before reveal initializes. The cover and
      // closing slides carry their own native resource panel instead, so they
      // are skipped here. Doing it here keeps the authored <section>s
      // untouched and the numbering self-maintaining.
      const sections = document.querySelectorAll('.reveal .slides > section')
      sections.forEach((section, index) => {
        if (index === 0 || index === sections.length - 1) return

        const badge = document.createElement('div')
        badge.className = 'qr'
        badge.innerHTML =
          '<img src="./qr.svg" alt="Scan to open the talk page">'
        section.appendChild(badge)

        const pageNumber = document.createElement('span')
        pageNumber.className = 'pagenum'
        pageNumber.textContent = String(index + 1).padStart(2, '0')
        section.appendChild(pageNumber)
      })

      Reveal.initialize({
        width: 980,
        height: 552,
        margin: 0.04,
        maxScale: 3,
        hash: true,
        keyboard: true,
        transition: 'none',
        controls: true,
        progress: true,
        center: false,
        plugins,
      })
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
